WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:06.800
I have to confess that I'm not a fan of sew-as-you-go, except maybe for blankets.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:12.200
But I was recently studying a vintage
pattern book and the light bulb came on.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.720
Now, if you aren't familiar with sew-as-you-go,

4
00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:18.240
one piece is knitted
and removed from the machine.

5
00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:22.800
As you knit the next piece,
you hang the outside edge of that first

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.920
piece on the edge stitch of the piece
you're knitting. When you're done,

7
00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.640
the pieces are joined
with no extra finishing.

8
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.040
But here's the light bulb moment.

9
00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:39.360
I've knit my first piece and I've
cast-on for the second piece.

10
00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:41.480
With the purl side facing,

11
00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.760
I'll pick up the edge of the first piece

12
00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:48.760
and hang it on the third
needle that's in work.

13
00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:53.880
Knit over and back.

14
00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:59.960
Pick up the next loop and hang
it on the third needle.

15
00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:07.400
Over and back and continue.

16
00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:12.920
Now, I know this looks odd.

17
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:19.000
Hang in there with me,
I'll continue picking up and knitting.

18
00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:43.480
When I get to the end, I'll hang
the final loop before binding off.

19
00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:56.520
This creates a nice corded or felled look.

20
00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:08.800
Remember, flat-felled seams
and sewing like jeans seams?

21
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.320
We're taking advantage of the Stockinette

22
00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.880
curl and creating a nice
texture for our garment.

23
00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:19.680
Can you think of someplace
you could use this technique?

